  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":56,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A park ranger measures the heights, to the nearest metre, of 200 trees at the edge of a forest reserve.\\n\\na Calculate the frequency density of the class 15 ≤ h < 20. (2)\\n\\nb The class 10 ≤ h < 15 is drawn 3.6 cm tall on a histogram. Find the height, in cm, of the bar for the class 25 ≤ h < 30. (1)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A park ranger measures the heights, to the nearest metre, of 200 trees at the edge of a forest reserve."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Calculate the frequency density of the class 15 ≤ h < 20. (2)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b The class 10 ≤ h < 15 is drawn 3.6 cm tall on a histogram. Find the height, in cm, of the bar for the class 25 ≤ h < 30. (1)"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Height h (m)"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequency"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5 ≤ h < 10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"18"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 ≤ h < 15"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"42"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"15 ≤ h < 20"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"70"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20 ≤ h < 25"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"48"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25 ≤ h < 30"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"22"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Class width = 20 − 15 = 5 m; density = 70 ÷ 5 = 14 (per metre). (b) fd(10–15) = 42 ÷ 5 = 8.4 ↔ 3.6 cm, so 1 density unit = 3.6 ÷ 8.4 cm ≈ 0.4286 cm. fd(25–30) = 22 ÷ 5 = 4.4, so the bar height = 4.4 × (3.6 ÷ 8.4) ≈ 1.89 cm.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying fd = f ÷ width, 1 for 14. (b) 1 mark for ≈1.89 cm (±0.01). Award the mark for 1.88–1.89 and for any answer derived consistently from the candidate’s own densities (scale factor method). Method credit: if the candidate correctly sets up the ratio but slips the arithmetic, 1 of 1 is not available here (single mark) — note the correct method in the report.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.9 cm (1 d.p.); ‘approx. 1.89 cm’.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":56,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Convert densities to a drawing scale with a ratio.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) frequency density","prompt":"Calculate the frequency density of the class 15 ≤ h < 20.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","The class width is 5; divide the frequency by it."],"answer":14,"tolerance":0.01},{"id":"b","label":"(b) bar height","prompt":"The 10 ≤ h < 15 class is drawn 3.6 cm tall. Find the height, in cm, of the 25 ≤ h < 30 bar.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Find the densities of the two classes and compare them.","Find both densities; the 10–15 class bar gets 3.6 cm, so scale the other bar in proportion."],"answer":1.89,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"cm"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Class width = 20 − 15 = 5 m; density = 70 ÷ 5 = 14 (per metre). (b) fd(10–15) = 42 ÷ 5 = 8.4 ↔ 3.6 cm, so 1 density unit = 3.6 ÷ 8.4 cm ≈ 0.4286 cm. fd(25–30) = 22 ÷ 5 = 4.4, so the bar height = 4.4 × (3.6 ÷ 8.4) ≈ 1.89 cm.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying fd = f ÷ width, 1 for 14. (b) 1 mark for ≈1.89 cm (±0.01). Award the mark for 1.88–1.89 and for any answer derived consistently from the candidate’s own densities (scale factor method). Method credit: if the candidate correctly sets up the ratio but slips the arithmetic, 1 of 1 is not available here (single mark) — note the correct method in the report.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.9 cm (1 d.p.); ‘approx. 1.89 cm’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Class width = 20 − 15 = 5 m; density = 70 ÷ 5 = 14 (per metre). (b) fd(10–15) = 42 ÷ 5 = 8.4 ↔ 3.6 cm, so 1 density unit = 3.6 ÷ 8.4 cm ≈ 0.4286 cm. fd(25–30) = 22 ÷ 5 = 4.4, so the bar height = 4.4 × (3.6 ÷ 8.4) ≈ 1.89 cm.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying fd = f ÷ width, 1 for 14. (b) 1 mark for ≈1.89 cm (±0.01). Award the mark for 1.88–1.89 and for any answer derived consistently from the candidate’s own densities (scale factor method). Method credit: if the candidate correctly sets up the ratio but slips the arithmetic, 1 of 1 is not available here (single mark) — note the correct method in the report.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.9 cm (1 d.p.); ‘approx. 1.89 cm’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table","statistics"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":"Use the statistics workspace to sketch the histogram if it helps."},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Class width = 20 − 15 = 5 m; density = 70 ÷ 5 = 14 (per metre). (b) fd(10–15) = 42 ÷ 5 = 8.4 ↔ 3.6 cm, so 1 density unit = 3.6 ÷ 8.4 cm ≈ 0.4286 cm. fd(25–30) = 22 ÷ 5 = 4.4, so the bar height = 4.4 × (3.6 ÷ 8.4) ≈ 1.89 cm.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying fd = f ÷ width, 1 for 14. (b) 1 mark for ≈1.89 cm (±0.01). Award the mark for 1.88–1.89 and for any answer derived consistently from the candidate’s own densities (scale factor method). Method credit: if the candidate correctly sets up the ratio but slips the arithmetic, 1 of 1 is not available here (single mark) — note the correct method in the report.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.9 cm (1 d.p.); ‘approx. 1.89 cm’."},{"n":57,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The ages, in years, of the 18 members of a tailoring cooperative in Porto-Novo are 24, 31, 27, 35, 42, 29, 24, 38, 33, 27, 45, 30, 24, 36, 41, 28, 33 and 39.\\n\\na Construct an ordered stem-and-leaf plot using stems 2, 3 and 4, and state its key. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ages, in years, of the 18 members of a tailoring cooperative in Porto-Novo are 24, 31, 27, 35, 42, 29, 24, 38, 33, 27, 45, 30, 24, 36, 41, 28, 33 and 39."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Construct an ordered stem-and-leaf plot using stems 2, 3 and 4, and state its key. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — Sorted ages: 24, 24, 24, 27, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 33, 33, 35, 36, 38, 39, 41, 42, 45.\\nStem 2 | leaves 4 4 4 7 7 8 9\\nStem 3 | leaves 0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9\\nStem 4 | leaves 1 2 5\\nKey: 2 | 4 means 24 years. n = 7 + 8 + 3 = 18 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (stem = tens, leaf = units, in years). 1 mark for the correct leaves on all three stems (or at least two of the three rows correct if one slip is made — use the workspace plot to confirm). The drawing itself is a construction: verify it in the stem-and-leaf workspace; marks above reward the assessable content.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — key phrased with any stem; leaves with any separators; ages listed in ascending order.","m":2,"df":"easy","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":57,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Order first, then group by the tens digit.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) key","prompt":"State the key of the plot (for example, what 2 | 4 means).","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["The stem holds the tens digit and the leaf the units digit.","State what one stem-and-leaf unit represents in this data set."],"accepted":["2 | 4 means 24 years","2|4 means 24 years","2 | 4 = 24 years","key: 2 | 4 means 24","2 | 4 means 24"],"keywords":[["means","=","represents","stands for"]],"minKeywordGroups":1},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) plot","prompt":"Write the leaves for stems 2, 3 and 4 (in order), for example ‘2: … ; 3: … ; 4: …’.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Sort the 18 ages into the three stems, lowest to highest.","Stem 2 covers the twenties — sort its leaves in the same ascending order."],"accepted":["2: 4 4 4 7 7 8 9; 3: 0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9; 4: 1 2 5","2 | 4 4 4 7 7 8 9 ; 3 | 0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9 ; 4 | 1 2 5","stem 2: 4447789, stem 3: 01335689, stem 4: 125","2: 4447789; 3: 01335689; 4: 125"],"keywords":[["4 4 4 7 7 8 9","4447789","4,4,4,7,7,8,9"],["0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9","01335689","0,1,3,3,5,6,8,9"],["1 2 5","125"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — Sorted ages: 24, 24, 24, 27, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 33, 33, 35, 36, 38, 39, 41, 42, 45.\\nStem 2 | leaves 4 4 4 7 7 8 9\\nStem 3 | leaves 0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9\\nStem 4 | leaves 1 2 5\\nKey: 2 | 4 means 24 years. n = 7 + 8 + 3 = 18 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (stem = tens, leaf = units, in years). 1 mark for the correct leaves on all three stems (or at least two of the three rows correct if one slip is made — use the workspace plot to confirm). The drawing itself is a construction: verify it in the stem-and-leaf workspace; marks above reward the assessable content.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — key phrased with any stem; leaves with any separators; ages listed in ascending order.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — Sorted ages: 24, 24, 24, 27, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 33, 33, 35, 36, 38, 39, 41, 42, 45.\\nStem 2 | leaves 4 4 4 7 7 8 9\\nStem 3 | leaves 0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9\\nStem 4 | leaves 1 2 5\\nKey: 2 | 4 means 24 years. n = 7 + 8 + 3 = 18 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (stem = tens, leaf = units, in years). 1 mark for the correct leaves on all three stems (or at least two of the three rows correct if one slip is made — use the workspace plot to confirm). The drawing itself is a construction: verify it in the stem-and-leaf workspace; marks above reward the assessable content.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — key phrased with any stem; leaves with any separators; ages listed in ascending order.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","stem_leaf","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":"Use the stem-and-leaf workspace to build the plot."},"marking":"Model answer — Sorted ages: 24, 24, 24, 27, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 33, 33, 35, 36, 38, 39, 41, 42, 45.\\nStem 2 | leaves 4 4 4 7 7 8 9\\nStem 3 | leaves 0 1 3 3 5 6 8 9\\nStem 4 | leaves 1 2 5\\nKey: 2 | 4 means 24 years. n = 7 + 8 + 3 = 18 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for a correct key (stem = tens, leaf = units, in years). 1 mark for the correct leaves on all three stems (or at least two of the three rows correct if one slip is made — use the workspace plot to confirm). The drawing itself is a construction: verify it in the stem-and-leaf workspace; marks above reward the assessable content.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — key phrased with any stem; leaves with any separators; ages listed in ascending order."},{"n":58,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The daily sales, in thousands of FCFA, of 14 shops in a market are 42, 55, 61, 38, 92, 47, 52, 45, 58, 64, 41, 49, 53 and 46.\\n\\na Show that 92 is an outlier, then draw a box-and-whisker plot for the remaining 13 shops, labelling its five-figure summary. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The daily sales, in thousands of FCFA, of 14 shops in a market are 42, 55, 61, 38, 92, 47, 52, 45, 58, 64, 41, 49, 53 and 46."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Show that 92 is an outlier, then draw a box-and-whisker plot for the remaining 13 shops, labelling its five-figure summary. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — All 14 values ordered: 38, 41, 42, 45, 46, 47, 49, 52, 53, 55, 58, 61, 64, 92. For the outlier test use the full set: n = 14, lower half 38, 41, 42, 45, 46, 47, 49 → Q1 = 45; upper half 52, 53, 55, 58, 61, 64, 92 → Q3 = 58; IQR = 58 − 45 = 13. Upper fence = 58 + 1.5 × 13 = 77.5. Since 92 > 77.5, 92 is an outlier. Remaining 13 values: 38, 41, 42, 45, 46, 47, 49, 52, 53, 55, 58, 61, 64. Min 38; Q1 = (42 + 45) ÷ 2 = 43.5; median = 49 (7th); Q3 = (55 + 58) ÷ 2 = 56.5; max 64.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the outlier argument (Q1 = 45, Q3 = 58, IQR = 13, fence = 77.5, 92 > 77.5). 1 mark for the five-figure summary of the remaining 13 (38, 43.5, 49, 56.5, 64). The box-and-whisker construction is verified in the statistics workspace: whiskers 38 and 64, box 43.5–56.5, median line 49.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘Q1 43.5, Q3 56.5’ in any order; drawings with the outlier excluded.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":58,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Split the data into halves for quartiles; test 92 against the fence.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) prove outlier","prompt":"Show that 92 is an outlier using the 1.5 × IQR rule.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["First include 92: order all 14 values and find Q1, Q3 and the fence.","Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR; then test 92 against it."],"accepted":["Upper fence = 58 + 1.5 × 13 = 77.5, and 92 > 77.5, so 92 is an outlier.","Q1 = 45, Q3 = 58, IQR = 13, fence = 77.5; since 92 > 77.5 it is an outlier.","92 exceeds the upper fence 77.5 (Q3 + 1.5 × IQR), so it is an outlier."],"keywords":[["77.5"],["92"],["fence","1.5","outlier","iqr"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) five-figure summary","prompt":"Give the five-figure summary for the remaining 13 shops.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Remove 92, then order the 13 remaining values.","Report five values in order: minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum."],"accepted":["38, 43.5, 49, 56.5, 64","38 43.5 49 56.5 64","min 38, Q1 43.5, median 49, Q3 56.5, max 64"],"keywords":[["38"],["43.5"],["49"],["56.5"],["64"]],"minKeywordGroups":5}],"marking":"Model answer — All 14 values orde